patslint: a linter for ATS2 source files
========================================

patslint checks the style of .sats, .dats and .hats files. It is an ATS2
program (no GC) built on libatsynmark and libatsopt: the text of a file is
lexed by the lexer of the compiler, so it knows which bytes are in
comments, strings and chars, or embedded C code (%{ ... %}), exactly as
patsopt does, and it can tell an unclosed comment or string. It works on
any ATS2 code; nothing in it is specific to this repository.

Build and install
-----------------

  make tools                          # the root Makefile: atsdoc, pats2xhtml, patslint
  make -f Makefile_devl bin_patslint  # patslint only (after `make all`)
  make -C utils/atslint               # the same, from this directory

They make bin/patslint and bin/patslint-diff; `make install` installs both.

Usage
-----

  patslint [option | file] ...

An option applies to the files that follow it on the command line. A
finding is printed as

  FILE: LINE(COL): SEVERITY(lint:RULE): MESSAGE

(LINE and COL count from 1; COL counts characters, not bytes), or as one
JSON object per line with --format=json. The exit status is 0 if no
finding has the severity of --fail-on (warning by default) or a higher
one, 1 if one has, and 2 if an option or a configuration file is wrong
or a file cannot be read.

  --list-rules          the rules and their default severities
  --enable=RULE,...     turn rules on (at their default severity)
  --disable=RULE,...    turn rules off
  --severity=RULE:SEV   off, info, warning or error
  --max-col=N           the maximal length of a line (default 80)
  --indent-width=N      the unit of indentation (default 2)
  --lines=N-M,...       report only the findings on these lines
  --all-lines           report the findings on all the lines again
  --fail-on=SEV         the least severity that makes the exit status 1
  --format=text|json
  --source              (text) each finding is followed by its line and a
                        caret under its column; the default when the
                        output is a terminal
  --no-source           (text) the findings only
  --semantic            also the rules after the binding of the names
                        (below; PATSHOME is needed)
  --fix                 fix in place: trailing-ws, crlf, final-newline,
                        blank-eof and the tabs of an indentation (to the
                        next multiple of 8); the other findings are
                        reported as usual

Rules
-----

The text rules apply to any file; the other ones to the ATS files
(.sats, .dats, .hats) only. `patslint --list-rules` prints them.

On the text:

  rule           default  what it reports
  tab            warning  a tab (a tab in a string or a char is not)
  trailing-ws    warning  white space at the end of a line (in a string
                          that goes on to the next line it is kept)
  line-length    warning  a line longer than max-col (a tab goes to the
                          next multiple of 8); a line that is one word (a
                          long string or name) or holds a URL is not
  crlf           warning  CRLF line ends (once in a file)
  final-newline  warning  no newline at the end of the file
  blank-eof      warning  blank lines at the end of the file
  indent         off      an indentation (of a line that starts with
                          code) that is not a multiple of indent-width
                          (info when it is turned on)
  non-ascii      off      a non-ASCII character outside comments and
                          strings (warning when it is turned on)

On the tokens (the lexer of the compiler):

  lex-error           error    a message of the lexer (an unclosed comment,
                               string, char or %{ ... %}, a character that
                               ATS does not have, ...); one for a line
  footer              warning  a comment (* end of [NAME] *) whose NAME, a
                               file name, is not the name of the file
  separator           off      a comment of stars that is not
                               (* ****** ****** *) (info)
  comma-space         off      a comma with the next token right after it
                               on the line (info)
  trailing-semicolon  off      a semicolon right before end, in, ), ] or }
                               (info; note that (e;) is of type void)

On the syntax tree (the parser of the compiler; a .hats file is parsed
as dynamic code, else as static code):

  parse-error     error    the first message of the parser (not when the
                           lexer reports an error)
  staload-dup     warning  a file staloaded again with the same name, at
                           the top level
  staload-unused  warning  a named staload (X = "...") whose $X does not
                           occur in the file (not in a .hats file)
  let-empty       off      let in E end (info)
  case-single     off      a case with one clause (info)
  case-unchecked  off      a case that is not case+ or case- (info)
  end-comment     info     an `end of [NAME]` comment right after a
                           function or an implementation, on its last
                           line, that names another function of the file
  fnx-template    warning  fnx on a template (the compiler makes it a fun)
  if-const        info     an if on true or false
  naming          off      a linear type (datavtype, vtypedef, absvtype)
                           whose name has no _vt suffix, or a view
                           (dataview, viewdef, absview) with no _v (info)

After the binding of the names (--semantic):

  unused-var     warning  a local val, var or pattern variable (of a case
                          clause, a val, ...) that is never used
  unused-fun     warning  a local function (in a let, a where or the
                          head of a local) that is never used; its own
                          recursive calls count as uses
  unused-arg     off      an argument of a function or a lambda that is
                          never used (info)
  shadow         off      a local name that hides a local name of an
                          enclosing block (info); a rebinding in the same
                          block, as in `val x = f (x)`, is not reported
  nonexhaustive  warning  a plain `case` (or the pattern of a plain
                          `val`) whose clauses do not cover all the
                          values, with the patterns that are missing
                          (three at most); the types may still exclude
                          them, as for a list_vt(a, 2): case+ is checked
                          by the compiler with the types, case- never

A name that starts with `_` is never reported. With --semantic, each ATS
file (not a .hats) is translated as patsopt does up to the binding of the
names (the parse, trans1 and trans2; the prelude is loaded once, which
needs PATSHOME), in a child process: a file that patsopt rejects (or that
needs -IATS paths) is skipped with a note on stderr, and does not change
the exit status. The tree of level 2 then gives the variable of every
name: the uses are counted, and a macro (macdef) counts as a use of the
variables of its body where it is used. It is slower (about 40 ms for a
file, most of it in the staloaded files).

Disabling rules in the source
-----------------------------

A comment (of any form) that holds

  lint: disable=RULE,...       disables the rules on its line and the next
  lint: disable-file=RULE,...  disables the rules in the whole file

RULE may be `all`. The text in strings does not count; the comments are
read in the ATS files only.

The configuration file
----------------------

The nearest file named .patslint in the directory of the checked file or
in one of its parents configures the check of the file (only that one is
read). Its lines are `KEY = VALUE`; `#` starts a comment line.

  RULE = off | on | info | warning | error
  max-col = N
  indent-width = N
  exclude = GLOB      # the file is not checked (it may be repeated)

A GLOB is matched (fnmatch, `*` matches `/` too) against the path of the
file relative to the directory of .patslint; a GLOB without `/` is also
matched against the name of the file. The options of the command line
take precedence over the configuration. The .patslint at the root of this
repository excludes the generated files.

The changed lines of a git repository
-------------------------------------

  patslint-diff [BASE] [patslint options]

runs patslint on the lines of the ATS files that differ from the merge
base of BASE (default origin/HEAD, else HEAD) and HEAD, the changes not yet
committed included; its exit status is the highest one of patslint. In
this repository:

  make lint [LINTBASE=origin/devel] [LINTFLAGS=...]  # the changed lines
  make lint-all [LINTFLAGS=...]  # the number of findings of each rule
                                 # in all the tracked ATS files

The CI runs `make lint` on the lines that a pull request changes.

Tests
-----

  make -C utils/atslint test     # the cases of TEST/suite.py
  make -C utils/atslint update   # rewrite TEST/expected/*.out from the
                                 # current patslint: review the diff

Each case writes its inputs (bytes, in suite.py) into a temporary
directory, runs patslint or patslint-diff there, and compares the exit
status, the output and the fixed files with TEST/expected/NAME.out.

Files
-----

  SATS/patslint.sats         the interface: the rules, the settings, the text
  DATS/patslint_main.dats    the command line
  DATS/patslint_text.dats    the masks of the tokens and the rules on the lines
  DATS/patslint_token.dats   the rules on the tokens
  DATS/patslint_tree.dats    the parse and the rules on the syntax tree
  DATS/patslint_sema.dats    --semantic: the translation and the rules on
                             the tree of level 2
  DATS/patslint_rules.dats   the table of the rules
  DATS/patslint_util.dats    the C part: the settings, .patslint, the output,
                             the text of a file, --fix
  CATS/patslint.cats         the declarations of the C part
  patslint-diff              the changed lines of a git repository (POSIX sh)

The no-GC rules are not lint rules: the compiler rejects the nonlinear
heap allocation itself.
